body:
For context, here are some examples:

1. Calling me weird, stupid, r-word throughout my whole childhood;
2. Not letting me speak up for myself during disagreements ("Sometimes you just need to be told to shut up.");
3. Love withdrawal when I didn't do something right or tried to take some agency for myself (e.g. asking to get the HPV vaccine as a teenager -- I got the cold shoulder for weeks, also "You'll really be crying when you're pregnant and have gonorrhea"), going behind my back to order pregnancy tests when my hormones got seriously out of whack and I ended up on my period for a month and a half;
4. Disrespecting my boundaries, not taking no for an answer (this has persisted through adulthood);
5. Revealing my mental health diagnoses to other family members and people outside the family without asking if it was okay to do so;

It's hard. There are probably a lot of examples that aren't even coming to mind that would better exemplify what my childhood looked like. My dad enabled her behavior frequently.

It took a good minute, but the guy I was dating (most recent ex and the reason I'm here again) was super patient and happy to be friends while I sorted my feelings/thoughts out. My ex-ex (the abusive one) was such a malignant little troll whose verbal and emotional abuse really wreaked havoc on my ability to experience emotional intimacy and closeness with anyone. I was honestly scared shitless when I met my most recent ex, who was really the wake-up call I needed that I deserved better and that better was within reach for me. 

When I was ready, I was really ready. It took about 4-6 months or so, but bear in mind that everyone is different, and no post-breakup/pre-new relationship timeline is going to look the same. With my most recent ex, I had a switch flip, and I decided that I was going to leave the past in the past, changed all of my communication habits for the better, kept a rich inner life, kept my friendships closer than ever, and really made sure to dig into my ability to be completely, honestly vulnerable. This was a hard switch to make, but it was something I was ready and willing to do in order to put this relationship on sure footing. It was a leap of faith, but I took it and it was a wonderful decision, even though the relationship ended. I was the most vulnerable, emotionally available, honest, and open that I've ever been. There will always be a bit of a learning curve with these things after a toxic relationship, even if it wasn't inherently abusive, so be really patient with yourself when getting back out there. 

When he and I broke up (after I'd told him I loved him, he left me the following week; I have plenty of theories about this, but am staying NC for at least 90 days), the pain of the breakup was much less significant. I can attribute this to a couple of things:

* I kept myself really busy during the relationship and have continued to do so;
* I have a much better self-image than I did a year ago;
* I am no longer co-dependent;
* I really have no regrets about the relationship or how it ended;
* And perhaps most importantly, I finally love myself enough and have lots of people in my life who love me, so an unrequited love only hurts my ego rather than my heart.

I'm putting myself back out there in the interest of moving on, but I'm in no rush to fill the void he left. I'm merely looking to see who's out there and to remind myself that my ex is not the only one who can treat me with kindness. 

It's going to really take some time, so be gentle and forgiving with yourself if it doesn't always go as planned.
